!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/37/io/ltr8/http/deployment.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/37/m/meta.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/37/m/core.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/37/m/policy.tn"
@doc:"""
  A deployment descriptor: how ONE INSTANCE is configured, as against what a document must be (a schema)
  or what an endpoint offers (an API description).

  [TSON-DATA] §8.2 introduced a security control with no artifact -- a restriction level and an optional
  script set over values, and over names a unit and a look-alike switch as well, enforced by default, with
  nothing said about where the configuration lives. This was the proposal for where, and Revision 37 adopted
  it: §8.2 now says no document may name, import or otherwise select the policy it is judged under, and the
  bundled policy.tn declares the policy's vocabulary, imported here. The short form of the argument is that
  the two obvious homes are both wrong, and for reasons stronger than the decision being orthogonal:

  A SCHEMA cannot hold it. An artifact that declared its own strictness would choose its own check, and a
  document carrying homographs would simply declare the level that admits them. §3.5 also makes a published
  schema immutable and §2.2.1 lets it be hash-pinned, while a policy must move -- confusables.txt updates,
  threat models change -- so raising a level would mint a new identity and every document pinning the old one
  would keep the old policy for good. [TSON-DATA] §8.3 settles it independently: skeleton distinctness does
  not compose across !!import, so the policy is a property of the merged namespace rather than of any one
  schema.

  An API DESCRIPTION cannot hold it either, being a schema here and inheriting both, and it would put policy
  in a contract -- raising a token policy would mean publishing a new description, which is the friction that
  gets a control switched off.

  THIS DOCUMENT IS DATA, NOT A SCHEMA, and the line is worth stating. An API description must be a schema
  because `request: order` is a type reference the resolver resolves; that is the whole argument for it. A
  descriptor references no types: a level is an enum member, a host is text, and a schema host is an
  identity, which is a URI. Nothing here needs a namespace, so nothing here needs to be a schema.

  Two rules hold, or self-certification returns by the back door:

    1. A processor is HANDED a descriptor; it does not go looking for one. A descriptor is diffable and
       reviewable where an environment variable is not, but a runtime that loads whatever is on its path
       still lets a container image change a security policy with no code diff.
    2. NO DOCUMENT MAY NAME ONE. There is no !!import of a descriptor and no reference to one from a schema
       or a data document. The moment a document can point at a descriptor, it picks its own enforcement.

  Rule 2 is why `acceptance_profile` below is a separate type rather than this one served as-is: what a
  counterparty may see is a PROJECTION, derived from what the processor enforces, never the descriptor.

  The policy a descriptor states is policy.tn's `policy`, COMPLETE, and applied exactly as written: what is
  in the file is what runs. There are no partial overrides falling back to the library's defaults, because a
  default is the library's to move, and a security setting whose effect can shift under a library upgrade
  with no diff to the descriptor is the silent relaxation §8.2 exists to prevent. A descriptor that states
  no policy leaves the library's defaults alone; one that states a policy states all of it.
  """
{
  @doc:"Where this instance listens. Absent members leave the caller's own defaults alone."
  listener => {
    host?: text
    port?: uint16
  }

  @doc:"""
    One deployment.

    `policy` is the processor policy this deployment runs under: policy.tn's identifier and token policies
    and limits, every part stated. Absent leaves the library's defaults, which point opposite ways round for
    reasons §8.2 gives -- a name is an interface and a homograph in one is an attack, while data may
    legitimately be a Cyrillic display name -- so a descriptor stating none is asking for exactly that, not
    for nothing. `unicode_data_version` is left out: the tables are the processor's, not the deployment's,
    and a descriptor naming one this processor does not carry is refused when it is read.

    Scripts are named by their [UAX #24] Script property alias, long (`Latin`, `Canadian_Aboriginal`) or ISO
    15924 (`Latn`, `Cans`), case-insensitively; a name that is not a script stops the read, so a typo in a
    security setting halts the process rather than being carried as a policy quietly missing a script.

    `schema_hosts` is the fetch allow-list: which schema identities this deployment will load over the
    network. Empty means none, which is a real posture rather than a missing setting -- a service that
    resolves only what it was given fetches nothing. It is deliberately NOT in `acceptance_profile`: a
    counterparty has no business knowing which origins this deployment trusts.
    """
  deployment => {
    name:          text
    listener?:     listener
    policy?:       policy
    schema_hosts?: [text]
  }

  @doc:"""
    What a counterparty may see: the answer to "what will this endpoint accept?", and nothing else.

    Served at a well-known path rather than at an identity's, and the reason is the same one that makes a
    descriptor unnameable. In this series everything with an identity is served at its identity's path
    (§2.2.1); a descriptor is precisely the artifact that must NOT have an identity, so a well-known path is
    right for it for the same reason it would be wrong for a schema.

    `policy` is the policy IN FORCE, in policy.tn's shape -- the shape `tson policy` and the CLI's report
    state one in. It is derived from the processor rather than copied from the descriptor, so a descriptor
    stating none still publishes the library's defaults, and the fetch allow-list is internal topology, so it
    cannot drift into it.

    A PROFILE IS A HINT AND THE VERDICT IS THE AUTHORITY. This can be cached, and a policy can change under
    it; only the 4xx a request actually receives says what applied to that request. The refusal itself
    carries only its code: §8.2 has the policy and the data version stated once per processor rather than on
    every refusal, and this profile is that statement for a counterparty. `unicode_data_version` is stated,
    as a report states it: §8.3 marks all three rules unstable across Unicode releases, so a refusal is only
    interpretable against the data that produced it.

    `limits` is here for the same reason the policies are: a 413 says a document went past a bound, and a
    sender that read the bound first never writes past it.
    """
  acceptance_profile => {
    name:   text
    policy: policy
  }
}
